  "id": "t13-surface-four-tools-only-and-registry-failsafe",
  "description": "T13 bilateral CLI observation (expected match), item 1 of the versioned inventory (contract decision 1/L1): the orchestration surface exposed to the model is exactly four verbs (spawn_session, steer_session, collect_session, delegate_task), not the seven the core itself exposes. cancel and list_children have no public tool surface at all (ADR-T13-02/03) — they must never appear as a tool NAME in the parent's own tool catalog, on either side. This is oracle-authored form (the exact tools array the oracle's own registry emits), so bilateral proves the oracle agrees, not just that the candidate matches its own belief about the oracle. Second half: dispatching an unregistered tool name (cancel, list_children, and a fabricated name) from inside a real turn falls through to the SAME generic registry failsafe both sides already use for any unknown tool (mirrors T09's own t09-registry-dispatch-errors/t09-failsafe-handler-catalog mechanism, applied here to the two specific names decision 1 says have no surface) — proving the absence is enforced at dispatch too, not just omitted from the schema. MEASURED (not just inferred) at manifest-authoring time from this scenario's own captured events.requests: both sides post exactly 24 tools per request, both include all four orchestration verbs, and neither includes \"cancel\" or \"list_children\" as a tool name anywhere in the catalog — the harness's own expectation primitives only support fixed-pointer equality, not an array-wide absence predicate, so that specific fact is recorded here rather than as a standing pointer expectation; the dispatch-failsafe half above IS pinned as a standing expectation.",
